== Semantic kernel ==

<pre>
Semantics
 └→ meaning / interpretation / truth conditions

Ontology
 ├→ philosophical: what exists; general kinds and relations
 └→ information science: formal specification of a conceptualization/domain
      ├→ Entity / Concept / Category
      ├→ Relation / Property
      ├→ Classification / Taxonomy
      └→ RDF / OWL / other encodings

Identity → sameness / persistence / re-identification
Concept → unit of thought / meaning
Category → kind / grouping
Classification → assigns items to classes/categories
Taxonomy → organizes concepts/categories, often hierarchically

Knowledge representation
 ├→ semantic networks
 ├→ rules / logic / frames
 ├→ ontologies
 └→ knowledge graphs

Schema → expected structure / types / constraints
Metadata → structured information about resources

Semantic Web
 ├→ RDF
 └→ OWL
      └→ Description logic

Inference → derives conclusions under a stated logic/model
</pre>

== Foundational separation ==

<pre>
world / domain
    ↓ conceptualization
ontology / schema / vocabulary
    ↓ encoding
RDF / OWL / other language
    ↓ reasoning
entailment / inference / classification
</pre>

These layers are related but are not identical.

== Normalized concept dossiers ==

=== Semantics ===
Study/specification of meaning and interpretation. In language it concerns semantic content, reference, composition, truth conditions and context; in formal systems it assigns interpretations/models to syntax.

'''Do not conflate:''' semantics ≠ syntax; semantics ≠ pragmatics; formal semantics ≠ all meaning; semantic theory ≠ metasemantics.

'''Sources:''' SEP "Theories of Meaning"; W3C RDF Semantics; formal semantics literature.

=== Ontology ===
Two related public senses must remain explicit:

# philosophy: study of what exists, ontological commitment, and the most general kinds/features/relations of beings;
# information science/AI: explicit or formal specification of a conceptualization/domain vocabulary, normally including classes/categories, relations/properties, constraints or axioms.

'''Do not conflate:''' philosophical ontology ≠ OWL file; ontology ≠ taxonomy; ontology ≠ schema; ontology language ≠ ontology content.

'''Sources:''' SEP "Logic and Ontology"; Gruber (1993), DOI 10.1006/knac.1993.1008; Studer, Benjamins & Fensel (1998), DOI 10.1016/S0169-023X(97)00056-6; ISO/IEC 21838-1:2021; ISO/IEC 21838-2:2021.

=== Entity ===
Anything treated as a distinct object of reference, quantification, description or representation in a discourse/model.

'''Do not conflate:''' entity ≠ physical object; entity ≠ database row; model entity ≠ proof of real-world existence; OWL individual and RDF resource are formal notions.

'''Sources:''' SEP ontology/categories; ISO top-level ontology standards; W3C RDF/OWL terminology.

=== Identity ===
Strict sameness: being one and the same thing rather than merely similar. Identity criteria govern persistence and re-identification.

'''Do not conflate:''' numerical identity ≠ qualitative similarity; identifier ≠ identity; co-reference ≠ guaranteed real-world identity; owl:sameAs is identity, not similarity.

'''Sources:''' SEP "Identity"; SEP "Relative Identity"; OWL semantics.

=== Concept ===
Unit/constituent of thought or structured meaning used in categorization, inference, memory, learning, communication and knowledge organization. Its ontology is disputed.

'''Do not conflate:''' concept ≠ word; concept ≠ class by definition; concept ≠ entity. SKOS Concept is intentionally flexible and does not settle the philosophy of concepts.

'''Sources:''' SEP "Concepts"; W3C SKOS Reference/Primer.

=== Category ===
Grouping or kind used to distinguish objects, concepts or phenomena. In philosophy categories can mean the most general kinds of beings or concepts.

'''Do not conflate:''' category ≠ class in every formalism; category ≠ taxonomy; category ≠ tag; MediaWiki Category is not automatically an ontological category.

'''Sources:''' SEP "Categories"; Aristotle; SKOS/RDFS as representational comparisons.

=== Classification ===
Process/system for assigning items, instances, observations or concepts to classes/categories according to criteria, rules, models or learned patterns.

'''Do not conflate:''' classification ≠ taxonomy; classification scheme ≠ classifier algorithm; statistical classification ≠ ontological classification.

'''Sources:''' W3C SKOS; ISO 25964; knowledge-organization literature.

=== Taxonomy ===
Organized system of categories/concepts, commonly using broader–narrower or parent–child hierarchy for classification/navigation.

'''Do not conflate:''' taxonomy ≠ ontology; taxonomy ≠ thesaurus; taxonomy ≠ nomenclature; parenthood must not silently mix is-a, part-of and topic nesting.

'''Sources:''' W3C SKOS Reference/Primer; ISO 25964.

=== Relation ===
Connection holding among two or more relata. Logic may model relations extensionally as tuples; ontology/KR uses named relation types among represented objects.

'''Do not conflate:''' relation ≠ property in every theory; relation instance ≠ relation type; transitivity/symmetry/inverses are semantic characteristics, not defaults.

'''Sources:''' logic/ontology literature; W3C RDFS/OWL. [[BITwiki:Relationships]] is a local application.

=== Property ===
Characteristic, quality or formally represented predicate attributable to an entity/class. In RDF a property is specifically a binary relation used as predicate.

'''Do not conflate:''' philosophical property ≠ RDF property; property ≠ database field; domain/range in RDFS are inferential semantics, not ordinary validation constraints.

'''Sources:''' SEP categories/ontology; RDF Schema; OWL 2 Primer.

=== Knowledge representation ===
Field/practice of encoding knowledge in explicit structures that support storage, communication, interpretation and computational reasoning.

Davis, Shrobe & Szolovits describe five roles: surrogate for the world; ontological commitments; fragmentary theory of intelligent reasoning; medium for efficient computation; medium of human expression.

'''Do not conflate:''' representation ≠ represented world; KR ≠ database storage alone; symbolic KR ≠ all AI.

'''Sources:''' Davis, Shrobe & Szolovits (1993), DOI 10.1609/aimag.v14i1.1029; Brachman & Levesque (2004).

=== Knowledge graph ===
Graph-based knowledge representation in which nodes/edges encode entities, concepts, relations, types, facts or other structured knowledge, often with schema/ontology and reasoning or learning layers. No single definition is universal.

'''Do not conflate:''' knowledge graph ≠ arbitrary graph database; knowledge graph ≠ ontology; RDF graph ≠ automatically knowledge graph; graph topology ≠ semantics.

'''Sources:''' Hogan et al., "Knowledge Graphs", ACM Computing Surveys 54(4), DOI 10.1145/3447772; contemporary KG surveys.

=== Semantic network ===
Graph representation whose nodes denote concepts/entities and whose links denote semantic relations. Historically important in AI/cognitive science and a precursor/relative of modern KGs and frame systems.

'''Do not conflate:''' semantic network ≠ Semantic Web; semantic network ≠ any labeled graph; semantic-memory models ≠ ontology languages.

'''Sources:''' Quillian (1968); Collins & Quillian / Collins & Loftus; KR history.

=== Schema ===
Explicit specification of expected/permitted structure of data, documents, databases, messages or knowledge artifacts, including fields, types, relationships and constraints.

'''Do not conflate:''' schema ≠ ontology; schema ≠ data instance; schema validation ≠ inference; RDF Schema is not a closed-world validation schema despite its name.

'''Sources:''' database theory; W3C XML Schema/SHACL/RDFS as contrasting examples.

=== Metadata ===
Structured information about resources supporting description, discovery, management, preservation, interpretation, rights, provenance or use.

'''Do not conflate:''' metadata ≠ only "data about data"; metadata can describe non-data resources; metadata vocabulary/schema ≠ metadata instance.

'''Sources:''' NISO "Understanding Metadata"; Dublin Core Metadata Initiative Metadata Basics.

=== Semantic Web ===
Web architecture/technology program for publishing and linking machine-interpretable data using global identifiers, shared vocabularies, formal semantics and standard data models/query languages.

'''Do not conflate:''' Semantic Web ≠ web pages plus AI; Semantic Web ≠ one database; Linked Data ≠ entire stack; machine-readable ≠ human-like understanding.

'''Sources:''' W3C RDF/OWL/SKOS specifications; Berners-Lee, Hendler & Lassila (2001).

=== Resource Description Framework ===
W3C framework/data model for representing information as graphs of subject–predicate–object triples and datasets, using IRIs, blank nodes and literals.

'''Current 2026 note:''' RDF 1.2 Concepts and Semantics are W3C Candidate Recommendation Snapshots dated April 2026. Core concepts should be version-neutral; 1.2 additions/status belong in a dated standards section.

'''Do not conflate:''' RDF data model ≠ Turtle/JSON-LD/RDF/XML serialization; RDF graph ≠ RDF dataset; RDF ≠ RDFS; RDF ≠ OWL; open-world semantics ≠ missing-is-false.

'''Sources:''' W3C RDF 1.2 Concepts; RDF 1.2 Semantics; RDF Schema; RDF 1.1 Primer.

=== Web Ontology Language ===
W3C ontology-language family for the Semantic Web with formally defined meaning, supporting classes, individuals, object/data properties, class expressions, axioms and automated reasoning.

'''Do not conflate:''' OWL ≠ RDF; OWL ontology ≠ philosophical ontology; OWL class ≠ OO class; owl:sameAs is strict identity; absence of assertion ≠ negation.

'''Sources:''' W3C OWL 2 Overview, Primer and Direct Semantics.

=== Description logic ===
Family of logic-based KR formalisms for concepts/classes, roles/relations and individuals, designed around well-defined reasoning problems with known decidability/complexity properties.

'''Do not conflate:''' DL ≠ one language; DL ≠ full first-order logic; expressivity trades against reasoning complexity.

'''Required topics:''' TBox/ABox; satisfiability; subsumption; classification; instance checking; consistency; OWL 2 DL and SROIQ relation.

'''Sources:''' Baader et al., The Description Logic Handbook; W3C OWL 2 Direct Semantics.

=== Inference ===
Process or relation of deriving/supporting conclusions from premises, evidence, rules, models or representations.

'''Do not conflate:''' inference ≠ truth; valid inference with false premises need not yield knowledge; entailment is a semantic relation while an inference procedure is an algorithm; soundness ≠ completeness.

'''Required distinctions:''' deduction, induction, abduction, probabilistic inference; RDF/OWL entailment regimes.

'''Sources:''' logic literature; W3C RDF Semantics; OWL 2 Direct Semantics; Brachman & Levesque.

